  "query_preview": "../Wednesday through Saturday/...\nAn active Fall pattern continues through the extended forecast. \nMaritime interests should remain aware of multiple Gale force \nfronts pushing across the Gulf and along the SEAK coast by \nThursday and into the weekend.\n\nWe pick up the extended forecast on Wednesday morning with the \nremnants of the Tuesday system pushing out of SEAK, scattered \nshowers and diminishing winds briefly through the afternoon. \nHowever, Wednesday will be the last \"quiet\" day of the extended \nperiod as multiple strong low pressure systems that will traverse \nthe North Pacific and then into the Gulf for the end of the week \nand into the weekend. Some variance on timing , but still thinking\nby Wednesday night a strong low develops in the northern Pacific \nand deepens to ~970mb by Thursday morning as it moves into the \nsouthern Gulf of Alaska. This low will swing a strong occluding \nfront across the gulf through Thursday, bringing another round of \nsustained Gale force winds 35 to 45 kts and storm force gusts up \nto 55 kts across the Gulf and along the coast from Dixon Entrance \nnorthward to the Fairweather Grounds by Thursday afternoon into \nThursday night. Strong overland winds up to 45 mph focused across \nthe southern panhandle by Tuesday afternoon, primarily Prince of \nWales, Ketchikan, and Annette Island so will need to keep on \npotential Wind Advisory Issuance moving into the middle part of \nthe week. This low and its attendant front continue to push into \nthe NE Gulf coast through Friday afternoon with scattered,off and \non showers becoming more prevalent into Friday night, so there is \na potential brief break in the widespread rainfall Friday night, \nalbeit not high confidence.\n\nTotal rainfall amounts from the front on Thursday into Friday \ntotal ~0.5 to 1.5 inches across the northern and central \npanhandle, and ~1.5 to 2.5 inches of rain for the southern \npanhandle. With the follow up systems from Friday night into \nSaturday night, rainfall amounts will once again be around the \nsame, maybe a bit higher given influx of available moisture tap. \nAll in all, the totals from Thursday through Saturday night, \naround 72 hours, are around 2 to 4 inches for the northern and \ncentral panhandle, 3 to 5 inches across the southern areas. Give \npersistent warmer southerly winds across the panhandle, any \nsnowfall should remain at the upper elevations of the Klondike \nHighway near White Pass and Haines Border Crossing. The further we\nget into the extended the less forecast confidence in placement \nof developing systems, but general consensus is that the pattern \nremains active with yet another surface low developing in the Gulf\nof Alaska on the periphery of a deep long wave trough situated \nacross the Gulf into latter half of the weekend.",
